$ |\n\nNext, we append the truth table for the second part in parentheses, $A \\lor B$. The statement $A \\lor B$ is true if either $A$ is true, $B$ is true, or both $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A \\land B\\,$ | $\\,A \\lor B\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nFinally, we look at the columns labeled $(A \\land B)$ and $(A \\lor B)$ to complete the column $(A \\land B) \\lor (A \\lor B)$. The statement $(A \\land B) \\lor (A \\lor B)$ is true if either $(A \\land B)$ is true, $(A \\lor B)$ is true, or both $(A \\land B)$ and $(A \\lor B)$ are true.\n\nThe completed truth table is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A \\land B\\,$ | $\\, A \\lor B\\,$ | $\\,(A \\land B) \\lor (A \\lor B) \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198094 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12701" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198094 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the statement $P \\land (Q \\lor Q)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198094 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e46add42-b23d-59cc-a611-a2f1b282d3bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198094 #edb/ref 1074 "The expression $Q \\lor Q$ simplifies to just $Q$.\n\nSo, the given statement can be simplified to\n$P \\land Q$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198095 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12703" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198095 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the missing entries in the following truth table?\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A \\land B\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198095 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e94e320d-c2d0-5ab4-b1e5-4e1e2c667bd5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198095 #edb/ref 1074 "The statement $A \\land B$ is true if both $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nThe complete truth table is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A \\land B\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198249 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198249 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198249 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198249 #edb/ref 1039 "Completing a Truth Table" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198249 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198249 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "21fd31e7-1eed-5dad-aff2-691ba1737b92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198250 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198250 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198250 #edb/ref 1039 "Completing a Truth Table for a Compound Statement" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198250 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198250 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f14ab339-1aa8-51a1-ac86-dc780120657c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198251 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198251 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198251 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying a Compound Statement" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198251 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198251 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bbe7854c-d438-5ee0-adc8-c32d5d800050" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198512 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198512 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198512 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198512 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198512 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198512 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198512 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198512 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198512 #edb/ref 1045 "Truth Tables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198512 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186199032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198512 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198512 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4b440e9b-6a56-5700-a008-c294f960b104" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198694 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198694 #edb/ref 1069 "q-170777" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198694 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the statement $(A∧A)∨(B∨B)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198694 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3b0fb093-40e7-5550-8cde-76d8edbb661f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198829 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186195894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198829 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186195897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198829 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186195900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198829 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182279" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198829 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $A$ | $B$ | $A∨B$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $T$ | $T$ | $T$ |\n| $T$ | $F$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $F$ | $T$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $F$ | $F$ | {{field-3}} |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198829 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ebfcb66e-44cf-500e-b596-5308bbe1f38d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198833 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186195949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198833 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186195952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198833 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186195955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198833 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186195958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198833 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182402" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198833 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $A$ | $B$ | $A∧(B∨(A∧B))$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $T$ | $T$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $T$ | $F$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $F$ | $T$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $F$ | $F$ | {{field-4}} |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198833 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b9848a17-e155-5b2d-8345-7d33a33f24e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199032 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199032 #edb/ref 1061 119963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199032 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e0c5d2cc-7c60-5845-95d4-cfaea7286f8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199032 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199033 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199033 #edb/ref 1061 119964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199033 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "af5b4487-0954-56d0-967a-c0db892f6793" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199033 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199034 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199034 #edb/ref 1061 119965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199034 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0440c492-e6e7-5adb-93a0-616d0582fb60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199034 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199035 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199035 #edb/ref 1061 119968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199035 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ea424b90-9a67-54e5-890d-eb71c3cebc21" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199035 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199036 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199036 #edb/ref 1061 119969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199036 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f0bf4b65-03a2-5b70-8097-f40d31d39e16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199036 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199037 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199037 #edb/ref 1061 119972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199037 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aeed77e3-d9c7-5a50-b4a2-1ca4802d3d55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199037 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199038 #edb/ref 1061 119973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199038 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "01942453-2001-5f5f-bec4-23750d4bbbe8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199038 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199518 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the statement \"$A$ and $B$\", represented symbolically as $A \\land B$, is true if $A$ is true *and* $B$ is true. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nWe can represent all possible permutations of the truth values of $A$ and $B$ and the subsequent truth values of $A\\land B$ using a **truth table**:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A \\land B\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nEach row of the truth table contains an input for $A$, an input for $B$, and the corresponding output for $A \\land B$.\n\n- The first row says that if $A$ is true and $B$ is true, then $A \\land B$ is true.\n- The second row says that if $A$ is true and $B$ is false, then $A \\land B$ is false.\n- The third row says that if $A$ is false and $B$ is true, then $A \\land B$ is false.\n- The fourth row says that if $A$ is false and $B$ is false, then $A \\land B$ is false." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199518 #edb/ref 1065 7963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199518 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ee98774e-d738-5b66-b2fc-e3c35ee94bf2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199518 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199519 #edb/ref 1064 "Two statements are **logically equivalent** if they have identical truth tables.\n\nA trivial logical equivalence is that every statement is logically equivalent to itself. We write this as follows:\n$P \\equiv P$\n\nTo prove this, we can use a truth table.\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,P\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nSince $P$ and $P$ have identical truth values, they're logically equivalent.\n\nAnother trivial equivalence is that the conjunction of a statement with itself is equivalent to the original statement.\n$P \\land P \\equiv P$\n\nFor example, the compound statement \"$x=3$ and $x=3$\" is logically equivalent to \"$x=3$\", which matches our intuition.\n\nAgain, we can prove the general result using a truth table.\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,P \\land P\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nThe statements $P$ and $P\\land P$ are logically equivalent since they have identical truth values.\n\nWe also have the following logical equivalence for the disjunction of $P$ with itself.\n$P \\lor P \\equiv P$\n\nWe can justify this result using a truth table.\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,P \\lor P\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nThe statements $P$ and $P\\lor P$ are logically equivalent since they have identical truth values.\n\nCollectively, the results\n$P\\land P \\equiv P,\\qquad P\\lor P \\equiv P$\n\nare known as the **idempotent laws.**" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199519 #edb/ref 1065 7976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199519 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "94d94958-eb9c-560a-8751-b05351806f2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199519 #edb/ref 1067 "The Idempotent Laws" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199600 #edb/ref 1064 "The statement \"$A$ or $B$,\" represented symbolically as $A \\lor B$, is true if $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or both $A$ and $B$ are true. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nThe corresponding truth table is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A \\lor B\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nAs we'll see, truth tables provide a convenient way to determine the truth values of complex logical statements. They can also be used to prove some mathematical theorems!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199600 #edb/ref 1065 9048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199600 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "69e8a334-8207-5b5e-a725-f336ddf61468" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199600 #edb/ref 1067 "The Truth Table of the \"Or\" Operator" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199601 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the following statement:\n\n$$\nA \\land (B\\lor A)\n$$\n\nWe can use a truth table to calculate the truth values of this statement given all possible permutations of the truth values of $A$ and $B$.\n\nFirst, we complete a truth table for the part in parentheses, $B \\lor A$. The statement $B \\lor A$ is true if either $A$ is true, $B$ is true, or both $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,B \\lor A\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nNext, we add a new column for $A \\land (B\\lor A)$.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,B \\lor A\\,$ | $\\,A \\land (B \\lor A)\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |  |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |  |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |  |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |  |\n\nTo fill in the rows, we consider the truth values of $A$ and $(B\\lor A)$ and use this to determine the truth value of $A \\land (B\\lor A)$ in each case.\n\n- Consider the first row. Since $A$ is true and $(B\\lor A)$ is true, their conjunction $A \\land (B\\lor A)$ is *true*:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,B \\lor A\\,$ | $\\,A \\land (B \\lor A)\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |  |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |  |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |  |\n\n- Next, consider the second row. Since $A$ is true and $(B\\lor A)$ is true, their conjunction $A \\land (B\\lor A)$ is *true*:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,B \\lor A\\,$ | $\\,A \\land (B \\lor A)\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |  |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |  |\n\n- Now, consider the third row. Since $A$ is false and $(B\\lor A)$ is true, their conjunction $A \\land (B\\lor A)$ is *false*:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,B \\lor A\\,$ | $\\,A \\land (B \\lor A)\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |  |\n\n- Finally, consider the fourth row. Since $A$ is false and $(B\\lor A)$ is false, their conjunction $A \\land (B\\lor A)$ is *false*:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,B \\lor A\\,$ | $\\,A \\land (B \\lor A)\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nThe completed truth table is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,B \\lor A\\,$ | $\\,A \\land (B \\lor A)\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199601 #edb/ref 1065 9049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199601 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9540705e-7502-5456-93aa-8987f69089ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199601 #edb/ref 1067 "Truth Tables of Compound Statements" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
